less than one percent of cars on the  road in the uk  are currently completely electric that  means a radical transformation is needed  not only of the charging infrastructure  but also the manufacture of electric  vehicles  i think it is possible to transform the  infrastructure if government  and local authorities are totally  committed to it  but without that commitment then a  target of 10 years  time is incredibly optimistic  transport is the biggest emitting sector  in the uk  generating a third of the uk's carbon  emissions  the way we travel and the kind of energy  we use are some of the biggest  changes britain will see in the next  decade  in an attempt to tackle climate change  the 10-point plan includes ending the  sale of new petrol and diesel vehicles  by 2030 instead of 2040  hybrid vehicles will still be sold until  2035.  wind nuclear and hydrogen will be key  components of a green grid  with plans for a town heated entirely by  hydrogen  by the end of the decade and the  government wants the uk to be a leader  in green technology  in particular carbon capture and storage  which  sucks carbon out of the air the  government says it's investing 12  billion pounds in its climate strategy  but analysts have told sky news only  four billion of that is new money  labor says the announcement falls short  of what's required  but the independent committee on climate  change which advises the government how  to get to net zero  has welcomed the plan i'm just so  pleased to see the prime minister  make a statement like this it's um it's  a really a bold statement  of ambition and um it's great to see a  prime minister of of britain set out a  vision like this  what's missing i suppose is the the road  map to achieving it  is this enough for britain to meet its  target of being net zero by 2050  it's not enough alone but my goodness it  will progress the issues  the pressure is on boris johnson to show  leadership on climate change  this plan is a start but it's a long  road to net zero by 2050  lisa holland sky news
 

